WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.057
A warm wind blew as the last train left the station.

2
00:00:05.058 --> 00:00:14.850
The dog, who had been waiting at the station since three o'clock, searched through the crowd of late-night travelers for his owner's familiar face.

3
00:00:14.851 --> 00:00:16.879
Where was his master?

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:23.775
The dog's master was never late, but tonight it seemed as though he wasn't coming home at all.

5
00:00:23.776 --> 00:00:32.531
Confused, hungry, and tired, the dog curled up in a corner of the station to wait for the morning trains.

6
00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:37.650
Hachi was born in northern Japan in November 1923.

7
00:00:37.651 --> 00:00:42.522
He was an Akita, a breed known for its loyalty and intelligence.

8
00:00:42.523 --> 00:00:48.330
Hachi was cream-colored, with pointy ears and an upturned tail.

9
00:00:48.331 --> 00:00:55.128
A few months after his birth, Hachi had been sent to Tokyo to live with Professor Ueno.

10
00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:59.049
Hachi and the professor had immediately become friends.

11
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:05.569
Each morning Hachi accompanied Professor Ueno to Shibuya Station.

12
00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:10.887
The professor would board the train and go to teach at Tokyo Imperial University.

13
00:01:10.888 --> 00:01:16.493
The last thing the professor saw as he got on the train was Hachi's wagging tail.

14
00:01:16.494 --> 00:01:22.653
The first thing the professor saw upon returning from work was Hachi welcoming him back.

15
00:01:22.654 --> 00:01:27.088
Hachi waited for the professor at the station every day.

16
00:01:27.089 --> 00:01:33.617
He had his own special spot from which he searched the crowds for his dear friend.

17
00:01:33.618 --> 00:01:38.206
Then Professor Ueno and Hachi would walk home together.

18
00:01:38.207 --> 00:01:44.252
This daily routine continued for over a year until tragedy struck.

19
00:01:44.253 --> 00:01:52.316
One morning in 1925, Professor Ueno went to work as usual but didn't return home.

20
00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:59.777
His heart failed, and he died suddenly at the university.

21
00:01:59.778 --> 00:02:08.714
Even though Hachi waited eagerly for his friend at the train station, the professor did not appear.

22
00:02:08.715 --> 00:02:17.539
The stationmaster tried to tell Hachi to go home, but there was no way to explain to the dog that his owner was never coming back.

23
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:21.937
The next morning Hachi waited at Shibuya Station.

24
00:02:21.938 --> 00:02:26.585
He continued to wait for weeks, and then months.

25
00:02:26.586 --> 00:02:31.932
The stationmaster felt sorry for Hachi and gave him food and water.

26
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:36.221
Professor Ueno's relatives came and took Hachi to their home.

27
00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:42.481
Nevertheless, the dog always found his way back to Shibuya Station.

28
00:02:42.482 --> 00:02:51.427
Every day, even in rain and snow, Hachi kept waiting, watching the trains, and hoping for his dear friend to appear.

29
00:02:51.428 --> 00:02:56.425
For the next ten years, Hachi continued showing up at the station.

30
00:02:56.426 --> 00:02:59.886
He sat at the exact same spot each day.

31
00:02:59.887 --> 00:03:06.877
Even though Hachi never saw the professor's face again, he always remembered where he was happiest:

32
00:03:06.878 --> 00:03:12.522
at Shibuya Station, waiting for his owner to arrive.

33
00:03:12.523 --> 00:03:21.738
Professor Ueno and Hachi had been together for only a short time, but the bond between them was unshakable.

34
00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:27.151
For people passing through Shibuya Station, Hachi became a familiar sight.

35
00:03:27.152 --> 00:03:29.750
They gave him food and water.

36
00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:35.531
Hachi allowed people to pet him, but his attention stayed focused on the trains.

37
00:03:35.532 --> 00:03:41.620
Then, in 1932, a newspaper printed a story about Hachi's loyalty.

38
00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:50.473
After that, people began adding "ko" to his name, which is a friendly, affectionate way of creating a nickname for a male.

39
00:03:50.474 --> 00:03:53.656
His story touched the hearts of many people.

40
00:03:53.657 --> 00:04:01.076
Soon people from all over Japan began visiting Shibuya Station to see Hachiko.

41
00:04:01.077 --> 00:04:05.022
They wanted to see this amazingly loyal dog.

42
00:04:05.023 --> 00:04:09.218
In March 1935 Hachiko's waiting ended.

43
00:04:09.219 --> 00:04:14.729
He died at his waiting spot at Shibuya Station.

44
00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:19.426
A famous Japanese artist had made a bronze statue of Hachiko.

45
00:04:19.427 --> 00:04:26.816
It was placed at the entrance of Shibuya Station, near the place where Hachiko last saw his dear friend.

46
00:04:26.817 --> 00:04:31.379
Unfortunately, during World War II, the statue was destroyed.

47
00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.049
But Hachiko was not forgotten.

48
00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:39.976
The original artist's son later made a second statue of the faithful pet.

49
00:04:39.977 --> 00:04:46.625
The bronze dog continues to wait at Shibuya Station today, looking toward the trains.

50
00:04:46.626 --> 00:04:51.895
Every spring there is a festival to remember Hachiko and his loyalty.

51
00:04:51.896 --> 00:04:55.714
People bring flowers to place at the feet of his statue.

52
00:04:55.715 --> 00:05:01.167
They hear his story and remember what a special dog Hachiko was.

53
00:05:01.168 --> 00:05:06.214
Shibuya Station is now one of the busiest areas in Tokyo.

54
00:05:06.215 --> 00:05:11.143
Hachiko's statue serves as a place for people to meet or wait for others.

55
00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:15.993
Some people pause and reflect on the meaning of Hachiko's story.

56
00:05:15.994 --> 00:05:31.828
In a hectic world, Hachiko represents what is truly most important in our lives: love and loyalty to those who are dearest to us.

